.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt RTC 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rtc
.Nd the CMOS real-time clock
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/rtc0
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The device is the machine's CMOS clock, which keeps the date and time while
the machine is off and which the kernel reads once at boot to set the time.
The kernel writes it only while an NTP daemon keeps the time synchronized,
as
.Xr adjtimex 2
describes.
It answers Linux's ioctls for reading and setting the clock and its alarm,
with a
.Vt struct rtc_time
of nine ints: the second, minute, hour, day of the month, month from 0,
years since 1900, day of the week from Sunday, day of the year from 0, and
a daylight flag, which is 0.
.Bl -tag -width RTC_WKALM_SET
.It Dv RTC_RD_TIME
Read the clock, as the boot does: two samples that agree.
.It Dv RTC_SET_TIME
Set the clock, in the encoding and hour format its status register gives,
with its updates held while the registers are written, and the day of the
week with them.
The time the kernel keeps is not changed; see
.Xr clock_settime 2 .
.It Dv RTC_ALM_SET , RTC_ALM_READ
Set and read the alarm's time of day, the second, minute and hour of the
struct; the clock raises its interrupt, IRQ 8, at that time each day while
the alarm's interrupt is enabled.
.It Dv RTC_AIE_ON , RTC_AIE_OFF
Enable and disable the alarm's interrupt.
.It Dv RTC_WKALM_SET , RTC_WKALM_RD
Set and read the wake alarm, a
.Vt struct rtc_wkalrm :
a byte that enables it, a byte that says it has come, and its date and
time.
The CMOS clock has no day alarm, so the time must lie within a day ahead,
as Linux's driver requires.
.El
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
of four bytes or more waits for the alarm and gives an unsigned long: the
alarms since the last read in the bits above the low eight, with
.Dv RTC_AF ,
0x20, and
.Dv RTC_IRQF ,
0x80;
.Xr poll 2
reports it readable once an alarm has come.
An alarm also ends a suspend to idle, which
.Xr rtcwake 8
starts through
.Pa /sys/power/state ;
see
.Xr sysfs 5 .
.Pp
The clock keeps UTC, as the kernel reads it, and two digits of the year, for
the years 2000 to 2099.
The device is root's to open, mode 0600, as Linux's is.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
A setting ioctl by a caller without privilege, as Linux answers it.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Dv RTC_SET_TIME
with a time out of range or a year outside 2000 to 2099; a wake alarm more
than a day ahead; a read of fewer than four bytes; or a
.Xr write 2
of the device.
The clock's update and periodic interrupts are not given.
.It Bq Er ETIME
A wake alarm in the past.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A non-blocking read with no alarm come.
.It Bq Er EIO
.Dv RTC_RD_TIME
of a clock that holds no time, such as one whose battery went.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another ioctl.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-util-linux 1 ,
.Xr adjtimex 2 ,
.Xr clock_settime 2 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
